Abstract
High-dimensional two-sample testing is challenging when the number of measurements
exceeds the sample size, measurements are dependent, and distributions have different
tail behavior. Mean- and rank-based procedures provide complementary evidence, but
their relative performance varies with distribution and signal structure. We propose three
permutation-calibrated tests that integrate variance-reweighted mean and rank order-statistic
statistics after mapping them to a common normal-score scale. The covariance-aware statistic
Hmah accounts for dependence between paired scores; Htail weights the two channels using
pooled kurtosis; and Hmax retains the larger calibrated score. Simulations under normal,
Student’s t, and skewed distributions show that rejection rates rise with signal strength.
The rank-based specialist is stronger under heavy tails and skewness, while Htail and Hmax
approach the stronger specialist. Hmah is more conservative in several settings, so no hybrid
dominates uniformly. In the GunPoint application, repeated subsampling of 15 observations
per class yields empirical rejection percentages of 74.2%, 79.0%, 58.2%, 80.0%, and 81.6% for
